id: design.bounded-queue
kind: design
title: Bounded queue
aliases: bounded channel, queue depth, buffer bound
tags: architecture, concurrency, reliability
questions:
  - why should a queue have a maximum size?
invariant: A queue has a finite capacity and a declared overflow policy, so latency and memory stay bounded under load.
answer_30s: A bounded queue caps in-flight work and turns overload into a decision -- block, reject, or drop -- instead of unbounded memory growth and unbounded latency.
answer_2m: |
  Queueing theory in one line: once arrival rate exceeds service rate, latency
  grows without bound and the queue becomes a memory leak with extra steps. The
  bound also determines what "healthy" means: queue depth is the best single
  saturation signal for a worker, and it is what you autoscale on when CPU is
  not the bottleneck. Choosing the depth is a trade-off between burst
  absorption (deeper) and failure speed (shallower): a shallow queue with
  shedding fails fast and keeps tail latency honest, a deep queue absorbs bursts
  but hides the moment the system stopped keeping up. Always name the policy:
  a bound without a policy just moves the crash.
when_to_use:
  - every hand-off between stages with different speeds, and every worker pool
avoid_when:
  - nowhere; if you believe you do not need a bound, name the overflow policy explicitly
commands:
  - kubectl top pod => memory flat under sustained load is evidence the queue is bounded
failure_modes:
  - unbounded queue, so a slow consumer becomes an OOM rather than a slow consumer
  - a bound so deep that latency budgets are breached long before the queue fills
  - no metric on queue depth, so saturation is invisible
follow_ups:
  - what would you autoscale on for a queue consumer?
related:
  - design.backpressure
  - design.worker-pool
  - design.producer-consumer
  - design.load-shedding
  - rust.tokio-mpsc
  - k8s.hpa
sources:
  - https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/avoiding-insurmountable-queue-backlogs/
